  "step_3": "First, the information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) includes the probability distribution: Lottery B has a 20% chance of 0€, which is a significant probability on the unfavorable end, making it right-skewed; Lottery A is deterministic and thus not right-skewed. The reason would prefer Lottery B. However, the individual does not mention skewness, tail probabilities, or distribution shape in their deliberation. Instead, they focus on expected value (which they compute), personal satisfaction with 3000€, and risk aversion due to non-repetition. There is no reference to the concentration of probability on unfavorable outcomes or the shape of the distribution. Second, the decision rule of the reason is to prefer the more right-skewed lottery (Lottery B), but the individual explicitly chooses the less right-skewed lottery (Lottery A) and justifies this choice based on risk aversion and utility, not skewness. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based reason to make the decision. The reasoning process is fundamentally different in both content and logic.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
